          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Turing Test</strong><br>\nTuring was also interested in artificial intelligence. He developed the Turing Test, a method for determining whether a machine can demonstrate intelligent behavior indistinguishable from that of a human. The test involves a machine attempting to fool a human into thinking that it is also human through conversation. This test remains a fundamental method for evaluating AI's progress.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-War Work and Advocacy for Computers</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Turing continued his work in computing. He advocated for the development of machines that could think and learn on their own. Turing was involved in the design of the Automatic Computing Engine (ACE) and contributed to early discussions on artificial intelligence. His vision for the future of computing was one where machines would be capable of doing any work that a human could do.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Prosecution and Chemical Castration</strong><br>\nIn 1952, Turing's life took a tragic turn when he was prosecuted for homosexual acts, which were illegal in Britain at the time. Given the choice between imprisonment and probation with hormonal treatment, Turing chose the latter, which led to chemical castration. This treatment was harsh and had severe effects on his physical and mental health.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
