          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nAlan Turing died on June 7, 1954, at the age of 41. His death was ruled a suicide, with the cause of death being cyanide poisoning. Turing's legacy is profound; he is remembered as a pioneer of computer science and a war hero. In recent years, there has been a significant reassessment of his life and work, and his contributions to computing and AI are celebrated globally.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Royal Pardon and Recognition</strong><br>\nIn 2009, following an internet campaign, British Prime Minister Gordon Brown made an official public apology on behalf of the government for the way Turing was treated after the war. Later, in 2013, Queen Elizabeth II granted Turing a posthumous royal pardon. This act acknowledged the injustice he suffered and cemented his status as one of the most important figures in the history of computer science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Modern Technology</strong><br>\nAlan Turing's work has influenced not just computer science, but the broader field of technology as well. Many modern devices, such as computers, smartphones, and the algorithms that run search engines, use principles that Turing developed. His ideas laid the foundation for the digital age and continue to shape the future of technology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
